AB  - 2022 to 2025, research on using AI for corneal disease classification has grown quickly,while AI for retinal diseases has continued to progress. Most studies on corneal disorders have focused on keratoconus, keratitis, and corneal ulcers. Advanced deep learning and machine learning methods, especially convolutional neural networks and transfer learning, have performed well on benchmark datasets, sometimes matching human graders. Still, clinical validation is limited.In comparison, retinal disease classification has been studied more thoroughly, especially for diabetic retinopathy, age-related macular degeneration, and diabetic macular edema.Leading models like MobileNetV2, Inception-ResNet V2, attention-based U-Net+ + hybrid ensembles, and Vision Transformers have achieved over 92% accuracy on some datasets, usually in controlled settings. Retinal studies benefit from larger and more varied datasets, while corneal research is more limited but growing fast. The literature shows that deep learning and machine learning methods have improved early disease detection and grading consistency on certain datasets. Some studies report better sensitivity, specificity, or AUC than traditional machine learning, but these gains depend on the dataset and often lack external clinical validation. Overall, these methods may be useful for large-scale screening in controlled environments, but realworld use is not yet confirmed.
