AB  - This study focuses on personalized eyeball structure modeling and parameter optimization algorithm for refractive surgery planning, aiming at improving the accuracy and safety of surgery by integrating multimodal medical image data and deep learning technology. Firstly, a personalized eyeball structure database including key parameters such as cornea, anterior chamber and lens was constructed, covering 500 clinical cases. Multi-modal data fusion is realized by graph neural network (GNN), and a personalized optical-biomechanical model of the whole eye is established to accurately characterize the optical and biomechanical characteristics of the whole eye. On this basis, a hybrid optimization strategy combining reinforcement learning (RL) and genetic algorithm (GA) is proposed to dynamically adjust the surgical parameters such as laser cutting depth, corneal flap thickness and aspheric correction coefficient, so as to minimize postoperative wavefront aberration and ensure the safety and biomechanical stability of corneal structure. The experimental results show that the root mean square value (RMS) of higher-order aberrations in the optimized algorithm group decreased by 48.1%, the MTF value increased by 35.5%, and the uncorrected visual acuity (UCVA) improved by 71.4% 6 months after operation, and the structural safety of the optimized algorithm group was significantly better than that of the traditional operation group. There was no case of corneal dilatation or RST below 250 μm, and the number of intraoperative parameter adjustments decreased by 87.5%. In addition, the optimized algorithm significantly reduced the incidence of postoperative complications, including refractive regression, corneal dilatation, night glare, dry eye and so on. In the intraoperative real-time performance test, the time of model reconstruction and parameter optimization was shortened to 1/38 and 1/227 of the traditional scheme, respectively, and the total decision-making time was accelerated by about 53 times, which met the needs of rapid decision-making during operation. This study provides an effective technical means for the accurate planning of refractive correction surgery and promotes the development of personalized ophthalmic surgery.
ER  - 

